'''Kwangju''' ([[Kwangeo language|Kwangeo]]: 광주; [[Darasian language|Darasian]]: 光州; [[Pinyin]]: ''Guāngzhōu''), officially the '''Socialist Autonomous Region of Kwangju''', is an [[socialist autonomous region|autonomous region]] in southwestern [[Daras]]. It borders [[Lamudalai]] to the west, [[Nanshan]] Province to the north, [[Bayanqing]] Province to the east, and the [[Southern Ocean]] to the south. The region covers an area of 184,240 square kilometres and has a population of 63,251,000, the majority of whom reside in the capital, [[Jinju]].

Kwangju is the homeland of the [[Kwangjuan people]], a distinct ethnic group within Daras. The region operates with a degree of domestic autonomy under the oversight of the [[Socialist Council of Daras]], with its own unitary parliament managing internal affairs. The [[Kwangeo language]] is the primary language of government and education, while [[Darasian language|Darasian]] is taught as a secondary language. Most Kwangjuans are bilingual.

The region was the core territory of the [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]], which capitulated to Darasian imperial forces in 924, becoming the last southern kingdom absorbed during Daras' southern expansion. Seorabeol retained substantial autonomy as a tributary state for nearly a millennium until its incorporation into the [[Socialist Republic of Daras]] in 1906 following the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]]. Kwangju's modern economy is defined by heavy industry and manufacturing, particularly shipbuilding, automotive production, and steel refining, though the region remains one of the most polluted in Daras and has undergone significant economic transformation over the past two centuries.

== Etymology ==

The name Kwangju derives from the [[Kwangeo language|Kwangeo]] 광주 (光州), meaning "province of light" or "radiant province." The name has been in use since the [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]] period and was retained through the tributary, autonomous, and republican periods. In [[Darasian language|Darasian]], the same characters are read as ''Guāngzhōu''.

== History ==

{{Main|History of Kwangju}}

=== Kingdom of Seorabeol ===

{{Main|Kingdom of Seorabeol}}

The [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]] was an independent polity centred on the territory of modern Kwangju. The kingdom emerged as a distinct political entity in the first millennium CE and developed a culture, language, and institutional tradition separate from the Darasian heartland to the north. Seorabeol maintained its independence through a combination of geographic distance, mountainous terrain along its northern frontier, and diplomatic pragmatism.

By the early 10th century, Darasian imperial expansion had reached Seorabeol's borders. In 924, facing the overwhelming military capacity of the Darasian imperial forces, the king of Seorabeol capitulated. The capitulation marked the end of Daras' southern expansion and the incorporation of the last independent kingdom on the frontier.

=== Tributary period (924–1906) ===

Following the capitulation, Seorabeol was absorbed into the Darasian empire but retained a degree of autonomy unusual among imperial territories. The kingdom functioned as a tributary state with nominal independence: it maintained its own royal house, internal administration, and legal traditions, while acknowledging Darasian suzerainty and rendering periodic tribute to the imperial court.

The arrangement proved durable. Successive Darasian dynasties found it more practical to maintain Seorabeol's semi-autonomous status than to impose direct administration on a distant and culturally distinct territory. The Seorabeol kings, in turn, avoided confrontation with the imperial centre and focused on preserving their domestic authority.

The relationship was not without tension. Periods of Darasian centralisation brought pressure for closer integration, and Seorabeol's cultural distinctiveness — its [[Kwangeo language|Kwangeo]]-speaking population, its Buddhist institutions, its separate aristocratic class — was a persistent source of unease in the imperial court. The most decisive act of cultural assertion during this period was the creation of the [[Garimgeul]] script in the 16th century, which formalised Kwangjuan linguistic identity as distinct from both Darasian and [[Lamudalai]] cultural spheres. Nevertheless, the tributary arrangement survived for nearly a thousand years, one of the longest-lasting such relationships in Darasian history.

==== Creation of Garimgeul ====

{{Main|Garimgeul}}

Prior to the 16th century, Kwangjuan was written exclusively in [[Darasian language|Darasian]] characters, a script whose complexity restricted literacy to the aristocratic and priestly classes. The court philosopher [[Bak Seong-rin]], serving in the retinue of the Seorabeol king, proposed the creation of a native script that would serve both as a practical instrument of literacy for the common population and as a marker of Kwangjuan cultural identity distinct from the Darasian and Lamudalai linguistic traditions.

Bak Seong-rin's treatise, presented to the court in the early 16th century, argued that a people who wrote in the characters of another could never fully express their own thought, and that the Darasian script — designed for a tonal language with a radically different grammatical structure — was ill-suited to representing Kwangjuan phonology. The practical argument was equally compelling: the complexity of Darasian characters meant that the vast majority of the Kwangjuan population was functionally illiterate, unable to read legal documents, Buddhist sutras, or royal proclamations without the mediation of a Darasian-educated scribal class.

The king approved the project, and Bak Seong-rin oversaw the design of the [[Garimgeul]] script — a phonemic writing system whose letters corresponded directly to the sounds of the Kwangjuan language. The name itself, from 가림 (garim, "to distinguish" or "to separate") and 글 (geul, "writing" or "script"), foregrounded the script's purpose: the delineation of Kwangjuan identity from the Darasian and Lamudalai cultural spheres.

The Darasian imperial court viewed the creation of Garimgeul with suspicion, interpreting it as an act of political separatism. The Seorabeol king defended the script as a practical measure for domestic administration, and the tributary arrangement proved sufficiently durable to absorb the tension. Garimgeul spread gradually through the population, first among Buddhist monasteries — which adopted it for the transcription of Kwangjuan-language sutras — and then among merchants and local officials. By the end of the tributary period, Garimgeul had become the primary written medium of Kwangjuan life, though Darasian characters remained in use for diplomatic correspondence and formal relations with the imperial court.

=== Coal and the industrial transformation (c. 1840–1904) ===

The tributary arrangement was transformed in the mid-19th century by the discovery and exploitation of the region's vast coal deposits. The northern mountains of Seorabeol contained some of the richest coal seams in the Darasian empire, and as Daras' industrialisation accelerated under Emperor [[Wenzong]], demand for coal grew precipitously. Private Darasian mining concerns, operating under licences granted by the Seorabeol court, opened the first major collieries in the 1840s. Within two decades, Kwangju had become one of the principal coal-supplying regions of the empire.

The economic effects were profound. Revenue from coal royalties and mining leases flowed into the Seorabeol treasury, transforming the tributary kingdom from a modest agrarian polity into a wealthy extractive state. The Seorabeol kings invested the proceeds in infrastructure — roads connecting the interior mines to the southern ports, improvements to [[Jinju]] harbour, and the construction of administrative buildings — but the core of the economy was extraction. Kwangju's coal fed the furnaces and factories of the Darasian industrial core, and the region's wealth derived from selling the resource to the imperial centre.

The mining boom brought Darasian workers and capital into the region in unprecedented numbers, shifting the demographic balance and creating social tensions that would persist into the 20th century. It also created a dependency: Seorabeol's prosperity was tied to Darasian demand, and any disruption to that relationship would have catastrophic consequences.

=== Darasian Socialist Revolution ===

{{Main|Darasian Socialist Revolution}}

The [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] (1904–1905) placed Seorabeol's position in jeopardy. King [[Hyeonjong of Seorabeol|Hyeonjong]], who had ruled Seorabeol since the late 19th century, had supported the reform programme of Emperor [[Taizong]] — the reforms that had precipitated the revolution — on the condition that Seorabeol's existing tributary relationship remain undisturbed. When the revolution erupted following Taizong's assassination, Hyeonjong negotiated with [[Chen Lixian]] and the [[Heavenly Emperor's People's Army]] to maintain Seorabeol's neutrality and autonomy.

Chen Lixian, seeking to avoid a two-front war while the revolution was contested, accepted Hyeonjong's terms. Seorabeol remained neutral throughout the conflict, contributing neither troops nor resources to either the revolutionary or preservationist factions. The arrangement allowed the revolutionary leadership to concentrate their forces against [[Yongzhe]]'s preservationists without concern for the southern frontier.

=== Incorporation into the Socialist Republic (1906) ===

Following the revolution's conclusion in 1905, the new socialist state moved to incorporate Seorabeol into the [[Socialist Republic of Daras]]. In 1906, the region was designated as an autonomous region, still under the domestic governance of King Hyeonjong, who was now formally subordinate to the [[People's Party]]. The arrangement was pragmatic: Hyeonjong was elderly, and many within the Party judged that the king's death would resolve the question of monarchical governance in the region without the need for confrontation.

Hyeonjong continued to govern with generally free reign over domestic affairs during this period, though the socialist framework was gradually introduced into the region's institutions.

=== Transition to autonomous republic (1911) ===

King Hyeonjong died in 1911. Following his death, the [[Heavenly Emperor's People's Army]] entered Kwangju to manage the transition. Hyeonjong was posthumously designated an "Ally of the Revolution" and a "Second Heavenly King" — honorific titles that recognised his contribution to the revolution through neutrality and incorporated his memory into the symbolic framework of the socialist state.

The region was converted from an autonomous region under a monarch into an autonomous republic with its own domestic management. A local parliament was established to govern internal affairs, overseen by a [[Socialist Council of Daras|Socialist Council]] liaison. The [[Kwangjuan people|Kwangjuan]] aristocratic institutions were dissolved, and the region adopted the Jianist governance model, though with modifications that allowed for Kwangjuan linguistic and cultural autonomy.

=== Post-revolutionary depression and transition (1911–c. 1940) ===

The transition from tributary kingdom to socialist autonomous republic was accompanied by severe economic dislocation. The dissolution of the private mining industry — the engine of Kwangju's 19th-century wealth — and its replacement with state-administered operations disrupted the regional economy at every level. The private Darasian mining concerns that had operated the collieries were nationalised, and the patronage networks that had sustained Kwangjuan merchants and suppliers collapsed.

The transition to oil as the primary energy source in the early decades of the 20th century compounded the crisis. As Daras' industrial core shifted from coal to petroleum, demand for Kwangju's principal resource declined sharply. The collieries that had made the region wealthy were now redundant, and the state-owned mining operations employed far fewer workers than the private enterprises they had replaced. The institutional change was massive: the economic structures that had governed Kwangjuan life for three generations were dismantled within a decade, and no adequate replacement was immediately forthcoming.

The region entered a prolonged depression. Unemployment rose, population growth stalled as young Kwangjuans migrated to the northern industrial cities in search of work, and Jinju's port traffic declined as coal exports dwindled. The socialist state, preoccupied with the consolidation of the revolution and the management of the national economy, allocated limited resources to the southwestern region during this period.

=== Industrial recovery and heavy industry (c. 1940–present) ===

The region's recovery was engineered through a deliberate programme of industrial reorientation supported by the national government. Beginning in the mid-20th century, Daras directed investment toward the development of heavy industry in Kwangju — shipbuilding, steel production, and automotive manufacturing — capitalising on the region's existing industrial workforce, its port infrastructure at Jinju, and its proximity to the Southern Ocean trade routes.

The strategy was to position Kwangju as a finishing centre within the Darasian industrial system. Raw materials and partially processed industrial goods were shipped from the northern production centres to Kwangju, where they were finished, assembled, and prepared for export. The finished products — ships, automobiles, steel components, manufactured goods — were then shipped from Jinju to markets in [[Nakamizu]], [[Okaiken]], and [[Lurandia]]. This role as a finisher of manufactured goods, rather than a primary producer, defined the region's economic position for the remainder of the century and into the present.

The recovery was gradual. By the late 20th century, Kwangju had re-established itself as a productive industrial region, though its economy remained middle-income relative to the Darasian average. The heavy industrial base that had restored employment and growth also produced severe environmental consequences: Kwangju is one of the most polluted regions in Daras, with air quality in the industrial corridors around Jinju consistently failing to meet national standards, and water contamination from mining runoff and industrial discharge affecting the river systems.

In recent decades, the regional government has pursued a transition toward greener technology. Electric vehicle production has been established at several plants in the Jinju industrial zone, and investment in renewable energy infrastructure has increased, though the pace of change has been constrained by the scale of the existing industrial base and the limited availability of capital for restructuring. Some growth in non-industrial sectors — services, information technology, logistics — has occurred, but the economy remains primarily a mix of heavy industry, manufacturing, and refining.

== Geography ==

Kwangju occupies the southwestern corner of Daras, bounded by [[Nanshan]] Province to the north, [[Bayanqing]] Province to the east, [[Lamudalai]] to the west, and the [[Southern Ocean]] to the south. The region covers 184,240 square kilometres.

The terrain is divided into three broad zones. The northern reaches of the region are dominated by the mountainous terrain along the border with Nanshan, part of the highland spine that separates the Darasian interior from the southwestern coastal territories. These mountains contain the coal seams and mineral deposits that drove the region's 19th-century extraction economy. The central zone consists of rolling hills and upland plateaus, which give way to the coastal lowlands in the south. The southern coastline is characterised by numerous peninsulas, rugged headlands, and irregular bays, creating a fragmented littoral with numerous natural harbours.

Several rivers flow southward from the northern highlands through the central hills to the Southern Ocean, providing water for agriculture and transport routes connecting the interior to the coast.

The climate is temperate, with cold winters and moderate summers. The region is considerably colder than the Darasian heartland, resembling the continental temperate conditions found in the more southerly latitudes of Fosia. Snowfall is common in the northern mountains during winter.

== Government and politics ==

{{Main|Politics of Kwangju}}

Kwangju operates as a [[unitary state|unitary]] parliamentary autonomous republic within the framework of the [[Socialist Republic of Daras]]. The region's domestic affairs are managed by the [[Parliament of Kwangju]], a unicameral body with legislative authority over internal matters including education, healthcare, cultural policy, and regional economic planning.

The [[Socialist Council of Daras]] maintains oversight through a designated liaison, who holds the authority to review and, in exceptional circumstances, veto parliamentary decisions that conflict with national policy or Jianist principles. In practice, this authority is exercised rarely, and Kwangju's parliament operates with a significant degree of autonomy.

The political parties active in Kwangju are the same as those in Daras proper. The [[Socialist Unity Party]] is the dominant party, followed by the [[General People's Party]]. Kwangjuan-specific political movements exist but operate within the socialist framework.

== Demographics ==

Kwangju has a population of 63,251,000. The [[Kwangjuan people]] constitute approximately 78 percent of the population, with [[Darasian people|Darasians]] forming approximately 18 percent and other groups the remaining 4 percent. The vast majority of the population is concentrated in the capital, [[Jinju]], which houses approximately 10 million residents.

Population growth in Kwangju continues at a rate above the Darasian national average, driven in part by larger average family sizes among the Kwangjuan population compared to the rest of Daras. The region's demographic profile is younger than the national mean, a consequence of both higher fertility rates and the historical pattern of out-migration that selectively removed older workers during the post-revolutionary depression.

The [[Kwangeo language]] is the primary language of government, education, and daily life. [[Darasian language|Darasian]] is taught as a compulsory secondary language, and most Kwangjuans are bilingual. Kwangeo is used in parliamentary proceedings, local courts, and regional media, while Darasian is used in communications with the national government and in inter-regional contexts.

Religiously, the population is divided between [[Buddhism|Buddhist]] practice (approximately 52 percent) and [[Irreligion|non-religious]] identification (approximately 38 percent), with the remainder comprising other faiths. The predominance of Buddhism reflects Seorabeol's historical religious tradition, while the high rate of non-religious identification reflects the influence of the socialist state's secular policies.

== Economy ==

Kwangju's economy is classified as middle-income within the Darasian framework. The region's economic structure is dominated by heavy industry, manufacturing, and refining, with a growing but still limited non-industrial sector.

The northern mountains contain significant deposits of coal, iron ore, and other minerals. Coal mining, once the engine of the regional economy, has declined substantially from its 19th-century peak but continues on a reduced scale under state administration. The transition from coal to oil in the early 20th century, and the nationalisation of the mining industry following the revolution, caused a severe economic contraction from which the region took decades to recover.

In the contemporary period, Kwangju's economy is oriented around its role as a finishing centre within the Darasian industrial system. Partially processed goods and raw materials from the northern production centres are shipped to Kwangju for finishing, assembly, and export. The principal industries are shipbuilding, automotive manufacturing (including electric vehicle production in recent years), steel refining, and heavy manufacturing. [[Jinju]]'s port serves as the primary export terminal, with finished goods shipped to [[Nakamizu]], [[Okaiken]], and [[Lurandia]].

Agriculture is limited by the temperate climate and hilly terrain. Grain production, primarily wheat and soy, is concentrated in the central upland valleys, but output is insufficient to meet domestic demand. Kwangju imports a significant portion of its food supply from other Darasian provinces.

Kwangju is one of the most polluted regions in Daras. The concentration of heavy industry, steel plants, and refining operations around Jinju has produced chronic air quality problems, and mining runoff continues to affect the river systems. Efforts to transition to greener technology — including electric vehicle production, renewable energy infrastructure, and emissions controls — have been pursued by the regional government, but progress has been constrained by the scale of the existing industrial base and the limited availability of capital for restructuring.

== Culture ==

Kwangjuan culture is distinct from that of the Darasian heartland, reflecting the region's long history as a separate polity and its [[Kwangeo language|Kwangeo]]-speaking tradition. The legacy of the [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]] — its royal institutions, Buddhist monasteries, and artistic traditions — remains a significant element of regional identity.

Buddhist monasticism, which flourished under Seorabeol, continues to play an important cultural role. The region's monasteries serve as centres of learning and repositories of Kwangjuan literary and artistic heritage, despite the socialist state's official secularism.

Kwangjuan literature, written in both Kwangeo and Darasian, has produced a distinctive body of work that blends the region's indigenous traditions with Darasian and socialist literary forms. The region is also known for its ceramics, metalwork, and textile traditions.

